<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Video Question Answering (VideoQA) based on Large Language Models (LLMs) has shown potential in general video understanding but faces significant challenges when applied to the inherently complex domain of sports videos. In this work, we propose <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">FineQuest</span>, the first training-free framework that leverages dual-mode reasoning inspired by cognitive science: i) <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Reactive Reasoning</span> for straightforward sports queries and ii) <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Deliberative Reasoning</span> for more complex ones. To bridge the knowledge gap between general-purpose models and domain-specific sports understanding, FineQuest incorporates <span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SSGraph</span>, a multimodal sports knowledge scene graph spanning nine sports, which encodes both visual instances and domain-specific terminology to enhance reasoning accuracy. Furthermore, we introduce two new sports VideoQA benchmarks, <span id="abstract1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gym-QA</span> and <span id="abstract1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Diving-QA</span>, derived from the FineGym and FineDiving datasets, enabling diverse and comprehensive evaluation. FineQuest achieves state-of-the-art performance on these benchmarks as well as the existing SPORTU dataset, while maintains strong general VideoQA capabilities.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>Sport Video Understanding, VideoQA, Training-free, Scene Graph
</div>
<figure id="S0.F1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.11796v1/teaser.png" id="g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/557;" width="476" height="557" alt="Refer to caption">
<br class="ltx_break ltx_break">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S0.F1.10" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>. </span><span id="S0.F1.11" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
(<span id="S0.F1.11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">a</span>) Constructed <span id="S0.F1.11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gym-QA</span> and <span id="S0.F1.11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Diving-QA</span> examples (Q1: easy, Q2: hard). (<span id="S0.F1.11.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">b</span>) Training-based VideoQA requires large annotated datasets for training but struggles to answer complex questions like Q2 (response from GPT-4V <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2024</a>)</cite>). (<span id="S0.F1.11.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">c</span>) Existing training-free VideoQA uses a VLM (captioner) and an LLM (reasoner) but fails on complex sports queries like Q2 (response from VideoTree <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>). (<span id="S0.F1.11.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">d</span>) <span id="S0.F1.11.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">FineQuest</span> combines Reactive Mode for simple queries and Deliberative Mode with Dynamic Motion Segmentation, Key Clip Selection, and SSGraph to address complex questions progressively.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Sports events captivate audiences worldwide, offering moments of intense action, strategic play, and athletic excellence. With the increasing demand for personalized and interactive viewing experiences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, Video Question Answering (VideoQA) has emerged as a promising technology to enable real-time insights and user-driven exploration of sports content.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, understanding sports videos presents unique challenges that go beyond those encountered in general video understanding tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2023a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>: ❶ <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Complex and rapid actions</span> require fine-grained motion understanding. ❷ <span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Multi-camera perspectives and multi-object interactions</span> demand sophisticated spatiotemporal reasoning. ❸ <span id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Strong contextual dependencies</span> necessitate domain-specific knowledge for accurate event interpretation. ❹ <span id="S1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Data scarcity</span> makes large-scale, high-quality sports video annotation prohibitively expensive, limiting the effectiveness of traditional tuning-based approaches (see Fig.<span id="S1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF00FF;"> <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ FineQuest: Adaptive Knowledge-Assisted Sports Video Understanding via Agent-of-Thoughts Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></span>(b)), which motivates the exploration of <span id="S1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">training-free VideoQA</span> methods.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Existing training-free VideoQA methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, while promising, face significant shortcomings when applied to sports videos (see Fig.<span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF00FF;"> <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ FineQuest: Adaptive Knowledge-Assisted Sports Video Understanding via Agent-of-Thoughts Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></span>(c)) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2020a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. Most rely on scene-based clustering <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hartigan and Wong, 1979</a>)</cite>, which struggles to handle the frequent camera switches inherent in sports broadcasts. Additionally, they often select a single frame or a small set of frames per cluster for captioning, which fails to capture the continuous and complex nature of sports actions. Furthermore, general-purpose vision-language models (VLMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jin et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2023b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Maaz et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma et al., 2023</a>)</cite> lack the specialized knowledge required to map visual cues to professional sports terminology (<span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, "what ‘626B’ refers to?"), leading to poor performance on domain-specific tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Adding to these challenges is the diverse complexity of sports VideoQA queries (see Fig.<span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF00FF;"> <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ FineQuest: Adaptive Knowledge-Assisted Sports Video Understanding via Agent-of-Thoughts Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></span>(a)).
For instance, a simple queries like "What sport is this?" rely on basic pattern recognition, while complex ones, such as "How many sub-sets of movements are performed?", demand multi-step reasoning involving segment identification, rule comprehension, and performance analysis. This highlights the limitations of one-size-fits-all reasoning approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2023a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, underscoring the need for a dynamic framework tailored to varying complexity and domain-specific requirements.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To address these challenges, we propose <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">FineQuest</span>, a novel training-free sports VideoQA framework that incorporates Agent Chain-of-Thought (CoT) planning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sanwal, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al., 2024</a>)</cite> as shown in Fig.<span id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF00FF;"> <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ FineQuest: Adaptive Knowledge-Assisted Sports Video Understanding via Agent-of-Thoughts Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></span>(d). Inspired by dual-process theories in cognitive science, FineQuest operates in two complementary modes of reasoning:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S1.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">➮</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reactive Reasoning</span>: Handles simple, low-complexity queries with a single-step process, leveraging pre-trained (M)LLMs for rapid responses.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">➮</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Deliberative Reasoning</span>: Tackles complex, high-context queries using multi-step reasoning guided by Agent CoT planning. For example, answering Q2 in Fig.<span id="S1.I1.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF00FF;"> <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ FineQuest: Adaptive Knowledge-Assisted Sports Video Understanding via Agent-of-Thoughts Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></span> involves: ① identifying relevant video segments via <span id="S1.I1.ix2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Dynamic Motion Segmentation</span>, ② selecting key clips based on the query using <span id="S1.I1.ix2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Key Clip Selection</span>, and ③ leveraging domain-specific knowledge for <span id="S1.I1.ix2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fine-grained Matching</span>. This mode systematically decomposes the query into sub-tasks and reasoning through multiple stages.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">This dual-mode reasoning framework ensures both effectiveness and robustness, allowing FineQuest to handle diverse queries with precision and adaptability. However, the lack of domain-specific knowledge integration is a major limitation of existing VideoQA systems. To bridge this gap, we introduce <span id="S1.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SSGraph</span> (see top of Fig.<span id="S1.p5.2.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF00FF;"> <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ FineQuest: Adaptive Knowledge-Assisted Sports Video Understanding via Agent-of-Thoughts Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></span>(d)), the first multimodal sports knowledge scene graph. SSGraph spans <span id="S1.p5.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">nine</span> sports (<span id="S1.p5.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span>, gymnastics, diving, basketball, soccer, ice hockey, tennis, baseball, badminton, and volleyball) and integrates: <span id="S1.p5.2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">(i)</span> <span id="S1.p5.2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Visual instances</span>: Representing key objects, actions, and attributes from sports videos. <span id="S1.p5.2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">(ii)</span> <span id="S1.p5.2.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Domain-specific terminology</span>: Encoding rules, scoring criteria, and contextual dependencies unique to each sport. SSGraph serves as a robust knowledge supplementation for reasoning, enabling FineQuest to answer questions that require a precise, context-aware understanding of sports scenes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Additionally, to address the limitations of existing sports QA benchmarks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>, which often feature limited query diversity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024a</a>)</cite> and lack coverage of varying intensities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, we build upon two high-quality action understanding datasets, FineGym <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2020a</a>)</cite> and FineDiving <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, to create Gym-QA and Diving-QA, for more diverse and comprehensive evaluation in the field as shown in Fig.<span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF00FF;"> <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ FineQuest: Adaptive Knowledge-Assisted Sports Video Understanding via Agent-of-Thoughts Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></span>(a). While the annotation process still requires specialized domain knowledge and cannot rely as extensively on (M)LLMs as recent works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mangalam et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ataallah et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, we introduce rule-based filtering procedures and leverage the advanced capabilities of LLMs to significantly reduce the burden on human annotators. Briefly put, our contributions can be summarized as:</p>
<ul id="S1.I2" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose <span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">FineQuest</span>, the first training-free framework designed to address the challenging task of sports VideoQA, integrating agent CoT reasoning with two modes: <span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Reactive Reasoning</span> for efficient performance on general or simple queries. <span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Deliberative Reasoning</span> for multi-step reasoning on complex queries.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To meet the specialized domain knowledge requirements for understanding sports videos, we develop SSGraph, the first multimodal sports knowledge scene graph, integrating domain-specific terminology and visual instances to enhance reasoning accuracy.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Extensive experiments demonstrate FineQuest achieves state-of-the-art results on our newly built sports VideoQA benchmarks, namely Gym-QA and Diving-QA, as well as existing SPORTU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia et al., 2024</a>)</cite> benchmark, while maintaining strong general QA performance.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsubsection">Sports Video Understanding</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">has evolved significantly, from early work on action recognition <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2020b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carreira and Zisserman, 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2018</a>)</cite> and gameplay phase categorization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cabado et al., 2022</a>)</cite> to broader tasks like player detection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vandeghen et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, tracking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goel et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, action spotting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vanderplaetse and Dupont, 2020</a>)</cite>, tactical analysis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Suzuki et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, and generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>. Datasets like FineGym <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2020a</a>)</cite>, FineDiving <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, MultiSports <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2022b</a>)</cite>, and soccer-specific datasets such as SoccerNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Giancola et al., 2018</a>)</cite>, SoccerDB <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, SoccerTrack <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Scott et al., 2022</a>)</cite> and UniSoccer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rao et al., 2024</a>)</cite> have driven these advancements.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Despite these advancements, sports VideoQA—a task that enables interactive video understanding—remains underexplored. Currently, only a few benchmarks exist for this purpose <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Specifically, Sports-QA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>, built on <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2020a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, offers limited query depth and diversity, as it primarily extends action labels into simple questions. While SPORTU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia et al., 2024</a>)</cite> considers query difficulty more thoroughly, all video clips in SPORTU are in slow motion, which limits its diversity and applicability to real-world scenarios.
To address this, we expand FineGym <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2020a</a>)</cite> and FineDiving <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2022</a>)</cite> to create Gym-QA and Diving-QA, introducing diverse query difficulties and supporting high-speed motion analysis, thereby filling key gaps in sports VideoQA research.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.11796v1/framework_new.png" id="S2.SS0.SSSx1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/242;" width="476" height="242" alt="Refer to caption">
<br class="ltx_break ltx_break">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>. </span><span id="S2.F2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
Deliberative Mode of <span id="S2.F2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">FineQuest</span>: Activated via a system switch for complex sports VideoQA tasks, it includes (1) Dynamic Motion Segmenter for adaptive sub-action segmentation, (2) Key Clip Selector for hierarchical contrastive decoding to identify query-relevant clips, and (3) SSGraph-based Fine-grained Matcher for precise query matching using structured instance-to-scene knowledge. </span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsubsection">Video Question Answering</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">generates open-ended textual responses based on video content and queries. Early work focused on short-term videos (5-30 seconds) using pretraining-finetuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, zero-shot <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2023a</a>)</cite>, and few-shot learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. Recent advancements have expanded its scope to longer formats like movies and vlogs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, supported by benchmarks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mangalam et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiao et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fu et al., 2024</a>)</cite> such as InfiniBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ataallah et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Concurrently, the emergence of LLMs has further inspired new approaches to VideoQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024c</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kahatapitiya et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. A prevalent strategy involves using VLMs to convert frame-level visual information into natural language, which is then processed by LLMs to generate predictions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSSx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSSx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Despite these advancements, existing models often neglect domain-specific challenges. Sports VideoQA, for instance, demands fine-grained motion analysis, multi-object interactions, and domain expertise. To address these gaps, we propose FineQuest, the first training-free framework tailored for sports VideoQA. FineQuest integrates agent Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning with two complementary modes: <span id="S2.SS0.SSSx2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Reactive Reasoning</span> for handling general and simple sports queries efficiently; <span id="S2.SS0.SSSx2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Deliberative Reasoning</span> for multi-step reasoning on complex, context-rich sports queries.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSSx3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsubsection">Knowledge Scene Graph.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Multi-modal knowledge graphs (MMKGs) enhance traditional knowledge bases by integrating visual and textual dimensions into standard triples (head, relation, tail) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ferrada et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2019</a>)</cite>. Examples include VisualSem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alberts et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, which enriches nodes with images and glosses, and PKG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2022b</a>)</cite>, linking Chinese poetry with visuals.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSSx3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSSx3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, MMKGs are limited to static knowledge and struggle to represent dynamic, context-sensitive scenarios like sports events. Knowledge Scene Graphs (KSGs) address this by incorporating spatiotemporal and contextual data to model interactions within events <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fei et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Building on this, we introduce SSGraph, the first sports-specific KSG, integrating multi-modal data and domain-specific knowledge to model dynamic interactions in sports. SSGraph bridges MMKGs and KSGs, enabling advanced reasoning for tasks like action recognition, quality assessment, and VideoQA.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The integration of components in visual agents <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> typically involves three key modules: a visual encoder <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{V}</annotation></semantics></math>, a natural language text encoder <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a large language model such as Vicuna <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chiang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. The process begins with the visual agent receiving a video <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> and an accompanying textual prompt <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, which could be a query or an instruction. These inputs are then processed into a unified multimodal representation space. Finally, the visual agent generates a textual response <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> based on the combined video and textual input. The generation process can be formalized as:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}=\mathcal{F}\left[\mathcal{E}_{V}(\mathcal{V}),\mathcal{E}_{L}(\mathcal{Q})\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}=\mathcal{F}\left[\mathcal{E}_{V}(\mathcal{V}),\mathcal{E}_{L}(\mathcal{Q})\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition</span> <span id="S3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(System 1 and System 2 Thinking) System 1 and System 2 are two distinct modes of thinking proposed by Nobel Laureate Daniel Kahneman in his book Thinking, Fast and Slow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kahneman, 2011</a>)</cite>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">System 1 - Fast Thinking (Reactive Mode): Unconscious, automated thinking processes that are fast, intuitive, and effortless. It is responsible for automatic responses and basic cognitive operations in daily activities. However, it is prone to heuristic biases and errors. Examples include recognizing familiar faces or locating objects in a room.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">System 2 - Slow Thinking (Deliberative Mode): Conscious, deliberate thinking processes that are slow, effortful, logical, and analytical. It handles complex calculations, reasoning, and decision-making while also monitoring and controlling System 1 processes. Examples include filling out a tax form or analyzing the structure of a sentence.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>SSGraph: Sports Knowledge Scene Graph</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A critical gap in sports video understanding compared to general video understanding is the lack of domain-specific knowledge in existing models. Addressing this through retraining or fine-tuning is challenging due to the diversity and scarcity of high-quality annotated sports data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To bridge this gap, we propose SSGraph, the first multi-modal sports knowledge scene graph, representing domain-specific knowledge across nine sports: <span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">G</span>ymnastics, <span id="S4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">D</span>iving, <span id="S4.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">B</span>asketball, <span id="S4.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">S</span>occer, <span id="S4.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">I</span>ce Hockey, <span id="S4.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">T</span>ennis, <span id="S4.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">B</span>aseball, <span id="S4.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">B</span>adminton, and <span id="S4.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">V</span>olleyball.
Each sports element in SSGraph is a tuple <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle n_{i},r_{j},n_{k}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle n_{i},r_{j},n_{k}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>, connecting nodes <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> via relation <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, capturing complex sports interactions.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsubsection">Raw Data Collection.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Unlike most MMKGs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alberts et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2019</a>)</cite> that focus on comprehensive knowledge (<span id="S4.SS0.SSSx1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, from Wikipedia), sports knowledge is specialized and structured, allowing much easier modification of sports disciplines. We constructed nine sports categories for new benchmarks, Gym-QA and Diving-QA, using datasets like FineGym <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2020a</a>)</cite>, FineDiving <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, MultiSports <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2022b</a>)</cite>, and internet sources.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsubsection">Knowledge Processing.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Specialized sports terminology poses challenges for understanding. We first collected videos corresponding to all sports elements (<span id="S4.SS0.SSSx2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span>, the finest-grained sub-actions) from the source datasets. Notably, the selection of videos requires adherence to specific criteria: ❶ <span id="S4.SS0.SSSx2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">high-definition quality</span>, ❷ <span id="S4.SS0.SSSx2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">minimal camera switching</span>, and ❸ <span id="S4.SS0.SSSx2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">good lighting conditions</span>, to better facilitate subsequent steps. Based on this, we meticulously watched each collected video multiple times to provide detailed descriptions of the athletes’ movements, serving as the general descriptions for each element action label. During this process, we also relied on external sources (<span id="S4.SS0.SSSx2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, websites<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            https://gymnasticshq.com/</span></span></span>) to ensure that our descriptions are accurate and professional.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSSx3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsubsection">Nodes Connection.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The structured nature of sports allows hierarchical construction of SSGraph as an MMKG (<span id="S4.SS0.SSSx3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, Gymnastic → event → set → element), connecting element nodes with video and description nodes.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSSx4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsubsection">Scene Representation.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSSx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSSx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To enhance spatiotemporal modeling, SSGraph is extended into a knowledge scene graph. A spatiotemporal scene graph (STSG) comprises sequences of individual scene graphs (SGs) for each video frame, capturing core semantic representations. Each SG contains "<span id="S4.SS0.SSSx4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">subject</span>"-"<span id="S4.SS0.SSSx4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">predicate</span>"-"<span id="S4.SS0.SSSx4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">object</span>" triplets, where "<span id="S4.SS0.SSSx4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">subject</span>" and "<span id="S4.SS0.SSSx4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">object</span>" represent two visual proposals (RoIs), and "<span id="S4.SS0.SSSx4.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">predicate</span>" defines the relationship between them, filtering less-informative details and aiding video content understanding. Each SG at frame <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{t}=(I_{t};E_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{t}=(I_{t};E_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, with sub-nodes <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v{t,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v{t,i}</annotation></semantics></math> connected by edges <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{t,i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{t,i,j}</annotation></semantics></math>. Temporal coreference edges <math id="S4.SS0.SSSx4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{t-1\rightarrow t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{t-1\rightarrow t}</annotation></semantics></math> link objects across frames, ensuring dynamic interaction tracking.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Methodology</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">FineQuest</span>, a novel training-free framework designed to address the challenging task of sports VideoQA. FineQuest features a dynamic system switch mechanism that enables rapid responses to simple sports questions or general questions (Reactive Mode) and accommodates deliberate reasoning for intricate questions (Deliberative Mode) as shown in Fig.<span id="S5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF00FF;"> <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Sports Video Understanding ‣ 2. Related Work ‣ FineQuest: Adaptive Knowledge-Assisted Sports Video Understanding via Agent-of-Thoughts Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a></span>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1. </span>Reactive Mode with System Switch</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Current works on visual agents for VideoQA primarily rely on visual question-answering datasets, which directly provide answers after inquiry as Eq. (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3. Preliminaries ‣ FineQuest: Adaptive Knowledge-Assisted Sports Video Understanding via Agent-of-Thoughts Reasoning" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF00FF;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). However, answering questions in this manner can compromise the reliability of responses <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Specifically, visual agents often hallucinate when handling sports-related questions that require deliberate reasoning, detailed visual understanding, and external domain-specific knowledge. To mitigate hallucination and enhance the reliability of the framework, we propose a system switch trigger mechanism that determines when deliberate reasoning is required. For a given question <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> and video <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define an MLLM as a Reactive Reason Agent <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\text{React}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>React</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\text{React}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>, equipped with the reasoning process to analyze the difficulty of the question:</p>
<table id="S5.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{\text{React}}=\mathcal{F}_{\text{React}}\left[\mathcal{E}_{V}(\mathcal{V}),\mathcal{E}_{L}(\mathcal{Q})\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mtext>React</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>React</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{\text{React}}=\mathcal{F}_{\text{React}}\left[\mathcal{E}_{V}(\mathcal{V}),\mathcal{E}_{L}(\mathcal{Q})\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, the reactive reason agent follows a structured reasoning process: ❶ <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Query Difficulty Analysis</span>: The agent first evaluates the difficulty of the question <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> based on the following dimensions: (<span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">a</span>) <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Question-Video Relevance</span>: Can the video directly answer the question? (<span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">b</span>) <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Question Type</span>: Static (<span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, action matching) <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">vs.</span> dynamic (<span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, event inference). (<span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">c</span>) <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Reasoning Requirements</span>: Single-step <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">vs.</span> multi-step inference. (<span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">d</span>) <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">External Knowledge Dependency</span>: Need for domain-specific knowledge. ❷ <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Decision Making</span>: Based on the difficulty analysis, the Reactive Reason Agent selects one of the following two actions: (<span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">i</span>) <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.16" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Direct Answer Generation</span>: If the question is determined to be simple, the response <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{\text{React}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mtext>React</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{\text{React}}</annotation></semantics></math> includes both the answer and the reasoning basis for the decision. (<span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.17" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">ii</span>) <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.18" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">System Switch</span>: If the question is deemed complex, the agent triggers a system switch, delegating the task to the Deliberative Reason Agent for advanced reasoning. The response <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{\text{React}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mtext>React</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{\text{React}}</annotation></semantics></math> includes a "switch" command and an explanation of why the switch is necessary.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2. </span>Deliberative Mode</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Previous training-free VideoQA paradigms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2023</a>)</cite> typically employ clustering algorithms (<span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, k-means <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hartigan and Wong, 1979</a>)</cite> in VideoTree <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>) to identify scene transitions and segment the video <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> into clips. The keyframes are selected based on the query <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a VLM acts as a captioner <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{Caption}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{Caption}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> to generate captions for these keyframes. These captions, along with <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, are provided as input to an LLM, which acts as a reasoner <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{Reason}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{Reason}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> to predict the answer.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While effective for general videos, this paradigm faces challenges with sports videos due to: (<span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">i</span>) Sports videos often feature single, continuous scenes with complex backgrounds, making scene-based segmentation ineffective. (<span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">ii</span>) Frequent shot transitions in sports videos are often misclassified as scene changes by clustering algorithms. (<span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">iii</span>) Keyframes fail to capture the full semantics of complex sports actions. To address these limitations, we further propose the Deliberative Reason Agent <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\text{Deliberate}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>Deliberate</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\text{Deliberate}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> to decompose the difficulty of the input query step by step.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsubsection">Dynamic Motion Segmenter.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To mitigate the ambiguity caused by complex backgrounds, we adopt the powerful SAM 2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ravi et al., 2024</a>)</cite> to highlight athletes in sports videos. This ensures the preservation of essential background context, enabling accurate captioning by the subsequent <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{Caption}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{Caption}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Given the lack of well-annotated sports video datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2020a</a>)</cite>, relying on pre-training with existing timestamp annotations may result in poor generalization. To address this, we propose the Dynamic Motion Segmenter <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{segment}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{segment}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>, which adaptively segments sub-actions without requiring predefined timestamps. This method segments sub-actions based on motion intensity, which naturally fluctuates during athletic performance. Specifically, athletes exhibit varying motion intensities, with brief pauses between action sets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2017</a>)</cite>. To capture these dynamics, we employ a sliding window to adjust the segmentation threshold based on local motion magnitude. This adaptive process aligns with natural human kinematics, enabling precise generation of sub-action proposals (details can be found in Algorithm <span id="S5.SS2.SSSx1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF00FF;">1</span> in <span id="S5.SS2.SSSx1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Appendix</span> §<span id="S5.SS2.SSSx1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF00FF;">B</span>):</p>
<table id="S5.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Proposals=\mathcal{S}_{segment}(\hat{\mathcal{V}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Proposals=\mathcal{S}_{segment}(\hat{\mathcal{V}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsubsection">Key Clip Selector.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given the segmented clips <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}[i]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}[i]</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{1,K\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{1,K\}</annotation></semantics></math>, the query <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> often pertains to only a small portion of the video. While prior works utilize VLMs (<span id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, CLIP in LVNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al., 2024</a>)</cite> or BLIP-2 in SeViLA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>) to directly measure frame-query similarity, this approach is prone to hallucinations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tong et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, caused by biases in training data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goyal et al., 2017</a>)</cite> and over-reliance on language priors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023a</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">To address this, we propose the Key Clip Selector <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{select}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{select}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>, which employs <span id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">contrastive decoding</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2022a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Leng et al., 2024</a>)</cite> to enhance relevance scoring by contrasting outputs from original and distorted inputs. Specifically, given a VLM-based selector parameterized by <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, the model processes <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> and a visual input <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}[i]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}[i]</annotation></semantics></math>, generating a response <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> sampled from the probability distribution:</p>
<table id="S5.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t}\sim p_{\theta}\left(y_{t}\mid\mathcal{C}[i],\mathcal{Q^{\prime}},y_{&lt;t}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t}\sim p_{\theta}\left(y_{t}\mid\mathcal{C}[i],\mathcal{Q^{\prime}},y_{&lt;t}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> augments the query <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> with a selection prompt <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{&lt;t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{&lt;t}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the sequence of tokens generated up to time step <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t-1</annotation></semantics></math>. To mitigate hallucinations, we introduce a distorted version <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{Dis}[i]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{Dis}[i]</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}[i]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}[i]</annotation></semantics></math>, producing two output distributions. Their differences are used to compute a contrastive probability distribution:</p>
<table id="S5.E5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S5.E5X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_left">(5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E5X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p(y\mid\mathcal{C}[i],\mathcal{C}_{Dis}[i],\mathcal{Q^{\prime}})=\operatorname{softmax}[(1+\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>softmax</mi><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p(y\mid\mathcal{C}[i],\mathcal{C}_{Dis}[i],\mathcal{Q^{\prime}})=\operatorname{softmax}[(1+\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.E5Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E5Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\operatorname{logit}_{\theta}\left(y\mid\mathcal{C}[i],\mathcal{Q^{\prime}}\right)-\alpha\operatorname{logit}_{\theta}\left(y\mid\mathcal{C}_{Dis}[i],\mathcal{Q^{\prime}}\right)]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>logit</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi><mi>logit</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>)</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\operatorname{logit}_{\theta}\left(y\mid\mathcal{C}[i],\mathcal{Q^{\prime}}\right)-\alpha\operatorname{logit}_{\theta}\left(y\mid\mathcal{C}_{Dis}[i],\mathcal{Q^{\prime}}\right)],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p2.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> controls the amplification of contrastive differences.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Unlike prior works focused on images <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Leng et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, our input consists of video clips, requiring distortions that preserve semantic integrity across spatial and temporal dimensions. We propose three types of distortions: ❶ <span id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Spatial</span>: Adding Gaussian noise to each frame. ❷ <span id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p3.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Temporal</span>: Applying temporal warping<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
                
                
                
              Distortion on the temporal duration of each frame while keeping the order unchanged</span></span></span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xing et al., 2023</a>)</cite> to adjust frame durations while maintaining order. ❸ <span id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p3.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Spatio-Temporal</span>: Combining spatial and temporal distortions. These methods avoid issues with approaches like CutMix <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yun et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, which can obscure fine-grained actions, or frame shuffling, which disrupts temporal coherence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>. For each clip <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}[i]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}[i]</annotation></semantics></math>, the distorted versions are denoted as <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{Spa}[i]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{Spa}[i]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{Tem}[i]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{Tem}[i]</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{ST}[i]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{ST}[i]</annotation></semantics></math>, with corresponding contrastive weights <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{ST}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{ST}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\alpha_{S}+\alpha_{T}+\alpha_{ST}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\alpha_{S}+\alpha_{T}+\alpha_{ST}</annotation></semantics></math>. The contrastive probability distribution is then reformulated as:</p>
<table id="S5.E6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S5.E6X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="3" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_left">(6)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E6X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle y_{t}\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle y_{t}\sim</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E6X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\operatorname{softmax}[(1+\alpha)\operatorname{logit}_{\theta}\left(y\mid\mathcal{C}[i],\mathcal{Q^{\prime}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>logit</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\operatorname{softmax}[(1+\alpha)\operatorname{logit}_{\theta}\left(y\mid\mathcal{C}[i],\mathcal{Q^{\prime}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.E6Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E6Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle-\alpha_{S}\operatorname{logit}_{\theta}\left(y\mid\mathcal{C}_{Spa}[i],\mathcal{Q^{\prime}}\right)-\alpha_{T}\operatorname{logit}_{\theta}\left(y\mid\mathcal{C}_{Tem}[i],\mathcal{Q^{\prime}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>logit</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>logit</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle-\alpha_{S}\operatorname{logit}_{\theta}\left(y\mid\mathcal{C}_{Spa}[i],\mathcal{Q^{\prime}}\right)-\alpha_{T}\operatorname{logit}_{\theta}\left(y\mid\mathcal{C}_{Tem}[i],\mathcal{Q^{\prime}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.E6Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E6Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle-\alpha_{ST}\operatorname{logit}_{\theta}\left(y\mid\mathcal{C}_{ST}[i],\mathcal{Q^{\prime}}\right)]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mi>logit</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>)</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle-\alpha_{ST}\operatorname{logit}_{\theta}\left(y\mid\mathcal{C}_{ST}[i],\mathcal{Q^{\prime}}\right)].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The clips <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{C}}[j]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{C}}[j]</annotation></semantics></math> with the top <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> relevance scores are selected, and adjacent clips are merged. The selected clips are then processed by the VLM-based captioner <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{Caption}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{Caption}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> to generate captions:</p>
<table id="S5.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(7)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{C}=\mathcal{P}_{Caption}(\mathcal{E}_{V/L}(\mathcal{S}_{select}(\mathcal{C},\mathcal{Q})))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐂</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{C}=\mathcal{P}_{Caption}(\mathcal{E}_{V/L}(\mathcal{S}_{select}(\mathcal{C},\mathcal{Q})))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{C}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the textual descriptions of the key clips.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSSx3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsubsection">Fine-grained Matcher.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The generated captions <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{C}={\mathbf{c}_{1},\mathbf{c}_{2},...,\mathbf{c}_{K^{\prime}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐂</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{C}={\mathbf{c}_{1},\mathbf{c}_{2},...,\mathbf{c}_{K^{\prime}}}</annotation></semantics></math> describe selected clips <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{C}}[j]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{C}}[j]</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in\{1,K^{\prime}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in\{1,K^{\prime}\}</annotation></semantics></math> in general natural language. However, such descriptions often lack the specificity required for answering sports-related queries due to the limited domain knowledge of existing LMs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSSx3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSSx3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To address this limitation, we propose the Fine-grained Matcher <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{match}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{match}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>, which matches domain knowledge from our constructed SSGraph <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{SS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{SS}</annotation></semantics></math> through a two-level fine-grained matching process:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSSx3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSSx3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">❶<span id="S5.SS2.SSSx3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cross-modal Instance Level.</span> At this level, we align captions and video clips with specific visual and textual elements, such as objects, actions, and attributes, within SSGraph. For each caption <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{c}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{c}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and its corresponding clip <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{C}}[j]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{C}}[j]</annotation></semantics></math>, we compute similarities with the textual description <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{d}_{n}^{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐝</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>S</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{d}_{n}^{S}</annotation></semantics></math> and visual instance <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{i}_{n}^{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐢</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>S</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{i}_{n}^{S}</annotation></semantics></math> of an action node <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{id}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{id}</annotation></semantics></math> in SSGraph, where <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\in\{G,D,B_{1},S,I,T,B_{2},B_{3},V\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\in\{G,D,B_{1},S,I,T,B_{2},B_{3},V\}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the sports category. Using Long-CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024c</a>)</cite>, which handles multi-modal data and long token sequences, the similarities are calculated via cosine similarity:</p>
<table id="S5.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(8)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="sim_{T2T}=\frac{\mathbf{c}_{j}\cdot\mathbf{d}_{n_{id}}^{S}}{\left\|\mathbf{c}_{j}\right\|\left\|\mathbf{d}_{n_{id}}^{S}\right\|},\quad sim_{V2V}=\frac{\hat{\mathcal{C}}[j]\cdot\mathbf{i}_{n_{id}}^{S}}{\left\|\hat{\mathcal{C}}[j]\right\|\left\|\mathbf{i}_{n_{id}}^{S}\right\|}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐝</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mi>S</mi></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐝</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mi>S</mi></msubsup><mo>‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐢</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mi>S</mi></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐢</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mi>S</mi></msubsup><mo>‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">sim_{T2T}=\frac{\mathbf{c}_{j}\cdot\mathbf{d}_{n_{id}}^{S}}{\left\|\mathbf{c}_{j}\right\|\left\|\mathbf{d}_{n_{id}}^{S}\right\|},\quad sim_{V2V}=\frac{\hat{\mathcal{C}}[j]\cdot\mathbf{i}_{n_{id}}^{S}}{\left\|\hat{\mathcal{C}}[j]\right\|\left\|\mathbf{i}_{n_{id}}^{S}\right\|},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.SSSx3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="sim_{T2T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">sim_{T2T}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="sim_{V2V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">sim_{V2V}</annotation></semantics></math> represent text-to-text and visual-to-visual matching, respectively. To mitigate ambiguity caused by minimal differences between fine-grained movements, we select the top five sub-actions from SSGraph with the highest similarity to each caption <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{c}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{c}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and clip <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{C}}[j]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{C}}[j]</annotation></semantics></math>. These are further matched across modalities using text-to-visual (T2V) and visual-to-text (V2T) processes, ensuring accurate alignment between captions and video clips. However, when textual or visual features are overly detailed, models tend to focus excessively on subjects, leading to hallucinations regarding relationships and interactions between subjects <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">An et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. This motivates reasoning at a higher relational level.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S5.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>. </span><span id="S5.T1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Main results on Sports VideoQA benchmarks. Bold values indicate the best performance and <math id="S5.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the corresponding improvement percentages over baselines (<span id="S5.T1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span>, LLaVA-Next-Video and Video-LLaVA). <math id="S5.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math> denotes that higher is better.</span></figcaption>
